{"kind":"stack","id":"fossils-stack","title":"How fossils are formed","theme":{"background":"#f5efe0","foreground":"#2b2b2b","font":"Georgia, serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-q","content":"What is a fossil?"},{"type":"image","picto":"bone"},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a","label":"The remains or traces of something that lived long ago","why":"Fossils are the preserved remains or traces of past living things.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"A new kind of rock made in volcanoes","why":"Pupils may confuse fossils with igneous rock, which forms from magma.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-c","label":"A stone that is alive and grows in rivers","why":"Pupils may think fossils are living stones, mixing up living and non-living.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Sediment"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"Small bits of sand, mud or soil that settle in layers, often at the bottom of rivers, lakes or seas."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Where do most fossils form?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"In layers of sediment at the bottom of water","why":"Sediment settles in water, where remains get buried and preserved.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"On the tops of tall mountains in the sun","why":"Pupils may think fossils form where it is dry and exposed, not buried.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Inside the hot centre of the Earth","why":"Pupils may think the deep Earth is where rock forms, mixing up fossils with magma.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-t","content":"You are walking by a river and spot a strange stone. Do you pick it up to study it, or leave it and keep walking?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Pick it up to study it","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Leave it and keep walking","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"You take the stone home. Inside you see the shape of a leaf pressed into the rock. You have found a fossil!"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You keep walking. Later you learn that the stone you missed may have held a fossil from long ago."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-q","content":"What must happen to a dead animal or plant for a fossil to form?"},{"type":"button","id":"c7-a","label":"It must be buried quickly in sediment","why":"Quick burial protects remains from decay and scavengers so they can be preserved.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"It must be left out in the open sun","why":"Pupils may think exposure preserves things, but sun and weather break remains down.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-c","label":"It must be burned in a fire","why":"Pupils may think heat preserves things, mixing up fossils with burnt remains.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"Petrified"},{"type":"text","id":"c8-e","content":"When minerals slowly seep into remains and turn them into rock, keeping their shape."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Over many years, what turns sediment layers into rock?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Pressure from the layers above squeezes them together","why":"Weight of layers above compresses sediment and cements it into rock.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"They are blown away by strong winds","why":"Pupils may think wind moves layers, not that burial hardens them.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"They melt in the heat of the sun","why":"Pupils may think sunlight melts sediment, mixing up melting with pressure.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which of these is the best example of a fossil?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"A dinosaur bone turned to stone in a rock layer","why":"A bone turned to stone in rock is a fossil, preserved from a past living thing.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"A stone picked up from a beach yesterday","why":"Pupils may think any stone is a fossil, forgetting it must come from a past living thing.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"A living tree growing in a garden","why":"Pupils may think living things count as fossils, mixing up living and preserved.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-t","content":"Well done! You finished the fossil quiz."},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"You now know how living things get trapped in rock and turn into fossils. Keep exploring the past!"}],"actions":[]}}
